cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  activityplan: onboard spacecraft activity planning with an auditable
  feasibility report naming the binding constraint
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-15
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  An onboard activity planner whose deliverable is a feasibility report rather
  than a timeline. It takes declared activities -- piecewise-constant power
  profile, data volume, priority, time window, precedence and exclusion
  constraints -- and a declared resource model -- battery capacity with a
  depth-of-discharge limit, an analytic eclipse-aware solar profile with a
  closed-form energy integral, a solid-state recorder, and contact windows with
  declared rates and transmitter power -- and reports the slack on every
  constraint in its own physical unit and normalised by a declared scale, with
  the binding constraint named. Two planners share one resource simulator: a
  priority-ordered earliest-feasible-start greedy baseline, and the exact
  optimum of the discretised problem by a time-indexed mixed-integer program
  over HiGHS. The exact optimum is cross-checked against exhaustive enumeration
  on nine small instances with zero disagreements, and the battery energy
  balance closes to 2.274e-13 watt-hours over twenty-one traces against a
  closed-form solar integral that agrees with adaptive quadrature to 4.743e-10
  relative. Over twenty-four seeded instances the greedy baseline reaches 95.11
  per cent of the proven optimum on average, 85.00 per cent at worst, and ties
  it on nine. A learned random-forest predictor of activity duration and energy
  with a split-conformal prediction interval is benchmarked against the analytic
  model on held-out activities: it wins, by a mean mean-absolute-error ratio of
  0.9249 on duration and 0.4116 on energy, and it wins because the analytic
  model omits two terms the declared synthetic generator contains, which is
  stated rather than elided. The interval attains marginal coverage on twelve of
  twelve level-mode-target combinations and fails conditional coverage,
  covering the longest third of activities 0.6782 of the time against a nominal
  0.90. Two negative results are reported in full. The learned warm start does
  not help the exact solver, because HiGHS closes the gap at the root node on
  thirty-one of thirty-four measured solves and a primal-bound cut has nothing
  to prune; what the learned predictor buys is that its candidate plan verifies
  as feasible on the target problem eight times out of eight against five of
  eight for the analytic model. And planning against the conformal upper bound
  on duration produces worse plans than planning against the point prediction,
  0.4167 against 0.4722 realised feasibility, because an activity's data volume
  is declared rather than predicted, so booking a longer duration books a lower
  production rate and the recorder overflows: duration robustness is
  anti-robust for the recorder, measured at a realised-to-planned rate ratio of
  1.1911 and an underbooking on 99.40 per cent of activities. Validation level
  3, research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for
  operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - activity-planning
  - mission-planning
  - oversubscribed-scheduling
  - mixed-integer-programming
  - spacecraft-power-budget
  - conformal-prediction
  - autonomous-aerospace-systems
  - feasibility-analysis
